RULES:
- Derive roles, routes, and page count ENTIRELY from the experience plan — build EVERY feature the plan describes
- CRITICAL: Each role MUST have visually DISTINCT pages — derive page types and sections from the plan, not from a fixed template
- Every route MUST retain surface and skeleton_id from its matching experience-plan page
- Every route MUST retain section_slots too; they must belong to the chosen skeleton contract, include its non-shell required slots, and exclude `shell`
- CRITICAL: Do NOT reorder section_slots into a generic hero → cards → CTA stack. The experience plan's slot order is the design recipe's rhythm — preserve it exactly so businesses don't share one mid-page silhouette.
- CRITICAL: component_file paths MUST reflect role folders: public pages in src/pages/, owner pages in src/pages/owner/, admin pages in src/pages/admin/
- Each route's files_to_generate instructions MUST copy the section list from the matching page in the experience plan
- CRITICAL: files_to_generate MUST have EXACTLY ONE "page" entry per route. Count your routes. Count your files_to_generate "page" entries. They MUST match — no exceptions, no merging, no skipping.
- `files_to_generate` is only the AI-owned page worklist. Include page entries and nothing else; router, theme, plumbing data, synthesized mock data, bridges, and the UI kit are written by later deterministic pipeline stages.
- Consumer / storefront products: include a public marketing landing at "/" or "/home" (layout "public", HomePage.tsx).
- BRIEF-FIRST ROUTES: Emit the routes/roles the experience plan and customer brief require. `product_kind` is chrome guidance + fallback only — do not drop brief-driven admin/staff/booking routes. Prefer the plan's inventory over any hardcoded industry page list.
- Hybrid products are allowed: public booking pages AND ops/admin pages in one architect when the brief needs both (e.g. clinic patient + front desk).
- INTERNAL desk products (hedge fund, trading, OMS, blotter): do NOT make a marketing landing the whole product. Primary "/" should be ops desk chrome. Forbidden as the whole product: hero + "Access Dashboard" / "Get Started" SaaS homepage.
- component_file paths must start with src/
- Do NOT include main.tsx or preview-bridge.ts (already in template)
- Quality bar: if a feature exists in the plan, there is a DEDICATED PAGE for it. Do not cram 3 features into one generic page.
- Nav, PublicLayout, AdminLayout, UiIcons, and every path under `src/ui/` are legacy or template-owned forbidden outputs. Catalogue pages own their single shell and use the immutable kit through `@/ui`.
- Page instructions must describe the LIVE product UX (what the customer/owner does on screen), never "demo this feature" or BMV pitch language. HomePage instructions must specify brand-voice hero + real storefront sections — not a list of preview_features as marketing cards.
- Trading briefs: prefer multiple ops routes (Desk, Ticket, Blotter, Positions, Risk) with dense data. Never ship a single HomePage as the whole product.
- AI features hub (`/ai-features`) must remain openable — never convert it into another ops-dashboard scaffold.
- Accounting briefs: prefer multi-page ops books workspace. Forbidden: marketing foresight engine landing as the only product.
- When `product_kind` is `saas_workspace` or `internal_ops`, primary product chrome should use OpsShell — do not emit public-home MarketingHero as the only app. Extra public routes are OK if the brief needs them.
- Every page instruction should demand production density: concrete widgets, realistic sample rows, and zero placeholder empty states.
- Visual quality: pages must feel cinematic and branded — never pale flat Tailwind starters. Instruct brand-token surfaces, recipe atmosphere, intentional motion, and dense real content. Ban gray SaaS / slate-900 / gray-50 defaults.